            "The long-acting reversible contraceptives — the implants and the intrauterine devices — are the most effective reversible methods available, with fewer than one pregnancy per 100 women a year in typical use, because once placed they do not depend on remembering a pill, finding money for a refill or reaching the clinic on the right week. Implants have become the second most used modern method in Ghana after injectables, driven by task-sharing to community health nurses and midwives, free provision under the National Health Insurance Scheme since 2022, and outreach. Both groups are safe in breastfeeding, in adolescents and in women over 40, both can be placed immediately after birth or abortion, and both return fertility immediately on removal — points that answer the fears that stop women choosing them. Their weaknesses are that they need a trained provider to start and to stop, that a woman who cannot find a remover is trapped with a method she no longer wants, and that the bleeding changes of the progestogen methods must be explained before insertion rather than after. The nurse who inserts must therefore be the nurse who removes, on request, without argument and without a fee for the removal itself.",
